{"id":"small-business-permits","title":"Sidewalk and small-business permits by neighborhood","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Are sidewalk tables, merchandise displays, food carts and parklets granted as readily in every neighborhood?","finding":"Of 21,196 such applications since 2015, 12.8% were denied, withdrawn, cancelled or voided (1.5% denied outright). After equalizing the mix of permit types the share not granted runs from 8.4% in Potrero Hill (736) to 22.3% in South of Market (975); 2 of 32 neighborhoods are beyond chance. Across 32 neighborhoods the link with income is -0.18 and with the share of residents born abroad +0.34.","how":"Public Works street-use permits of six small-business types (Food truck or cart, Free-sample stand, Minor sidewalk encroachment, Parklet, Sidewalk merchandise display, Sidewalk tables and chairs) with a final status. “Not granted” = denied, withdrawn, cancelled or void. Each neighborhood’s rate is standardized to the citywide mix of the six types; neighborhoods with under 50 applications are left out.","limits":"Sidewalk width, transit stops and street slope decide many of these, and they differ by neighborhood. “Void” covers clerical duplicates as well as refusals. Entertainment, cannabis and tables-and-chairs registers on disk list only permits now active, so refusals in those programs cannot be counted. A difference between places is not proof of bias. Places differ in what is proposed, in zoning, lot sizes, building ages and land prices, and in who chooses to apply at all; applicants also withdraw for their own reasons (money, a sale, a redesign filed under a new number). The hearing minutes, roll-call votes and appeal decisions now being collected would show who voted, who objected and what reason was recorded in each case.","columns":[{"k":"area","t":"Neighborhood","type":"text"},{"k":"applications","t":"Applications","type":"int"},{"k":"notGranted","t":"Not granted","type":"pct"},{"k":"adjusted","t":"Not granted, same mix of 